{"causes": ["The relentless southward expansion and military pressure from the Nguyễn Lords of Đại Việt compelled Champa to seek strong leadership.", "Internal political fragmentation and dynastic instability within Champa before his ascent created a vacuum for a unifying figure.", "The growing influence of Islam in Southeast Asia, particularly among Cham communities, necessitated a ruler capable of integrating diverse religious identities."], "effects": ["Po Rome's reign temporarily halted the aggressive southward expansion of Đại Việt into Cham territories, preserving Champa's independence for a critical period.", "He fostered a unique form of religious syncretism within Champa, effectively integrating Islam with traditional indigenous Cham beliefs and practices.", "His leadership strengthened Cham political and cultural identity, establishing him as an enduring symbol of resistance against external domination."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "islam", "sourceName": "Islam", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "po-rome", "targetName": "Po Rome", "context": "Po Rome, though also preserving traditional Cham beliefs, adopted a Muslim name and title, reflecting Islam's growing influence in his court and kingdom."}, {"sourceSlug": "po-rome", "sourceName": "Po Rome", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "panduranga-champa", "targetName": "Panduranga Champa", "context": "Po Rome reigned as the king of Panduranga Champa, the last significant remnant of the historical Cham kingdoms, from 1627 to 1651."}, {"sourceSlug": "po-rome", "sourceName": "Po Rome", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "cham-dai-viet-wars", "targetName": "Cham-Dai Viet Wars", "context": "Po Rome actively engaged in military conflicts and diplomatic maneuvers against the Nguyễn Lords of Đại Việt, defending Cham sovereignty during his reign."}, {"sourceSlug": "nguyen-lords", "sourceName": "Nguyễn Lords", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "po-rome", "targetName": "Po Rome", "context": "The expansionist policies and military pressure from the Nguyễn Lords were a primary catalyst for Po Rome's consolidation of power and focus on strengthening Cham defenses."}, {"sourceSlug": "po-rome", "sourceName": "Po Rome", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "cham-culture", "targetName": "Cham Culture", "context": "Po Rome's policies of religious tolerance and syncretism profoundly influenced the distinctive spiritual and cultural identity of the Cham people for centuries."}], "places": [{"name": "Panduranga, Champa", "role": "Kingdom's Center"}, {"name": "Phan Rang, Vietnam", "role": "Modern Capital Site"}, {"name": "Đại Việt", "role": "Rival Neighbor"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Po Rome", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "po-rome"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q59155457", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Po_Rome"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Po Rome's reign provided critical stability for Champa amidst intense external pressures, temporarily halting Đại Việt's expansion and forging a unique religious syncretism that shaped Cham identity for centuries.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-12T23:23:29Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29213244688", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Po Rome (?–1651), also spelled Po Romê, Po Romé or Po Ramo, with the Muslim name Nik Mustafa Bin Wan Abul Muzaffar Waliyullah, regnal name Sultan Abdul Hamid Shah, was the king of Panduranga Champa, reigning from 1627 to 1651.", "newValue": "Po Rome (c. 1599–1651), also known by his Muslim name Nik Mustafa Bin Wan Abul Muzaffar Waliyullah and the regnal title Sultan Abdul Hamid Shah, was the revered king of Panduranga Champa, reigning from 1627 until his death in 1651. His rule marked a critical period for the Cham people, characterized by a determined effort to consolidate state power, resist the increasing southward expansion of Đại Việt, and foster a unique synthesis of Cham religious traditions, integrating Islam while respecting indigenous beliefs. He is remembered as one of the last great Cham rulers who managed to provide a measure of stability and cultural vibrancy amidst mounting external pressures.

During his reign, Po Rome implemented significant administrative and military reforms, strengthening the Cham kingdom's defenses against the Nguyễn Lords of Đại Việt. He engaged in both diplomatic and military confrontations, notably halting, at least temporarily, further territorial encroachment from the Vietnamese. Internally, Po Rome navigated complex religious dynamics, personally embracing Islam yet actively promoting religious tolerance and syncretism. This policy saw the construction of both mosques and traditional Cham temples (Akar Thrah), reflecting a deliberate strategy to unify his diverse populace under a shared Cham identity that transcended specific religious affiliations.

Po Rome's legacy profoundly shaped Cham identity and historical memory, establishing him as a symbol of resistance and cultural resilience. His reign is often regarded as a "golden age" for Champa, a period when the kingdom, despite its diminished size, exerted considerable influence and maintained its sovereignty against formidable neighbors. The religious syncretism he championed continued to define Cham spiritual life for centuries, influencing the distinct practices of both Cham Bani (Muslims) and Cham Ahier (traditionalists)."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-12T23:23:29Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29213244688", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The relentless southward expansion and military pressure from the Nguyễn Lords of Đại Việt compelled Champa to seek strong leadership.", "Internal political fragmentation and dynastic instability within Champa before his ascent created a vacuum for a unifying figure.", "The growing influence of Islam in Southeast Asia, particularly among Cham communities, necessitated a ruler capable of integrating diverse religious identities."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-12T23:23:29Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29213244688", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1651"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1651"}], "newValue": ["Po Rome's reign temporarily halted the aggressive southward expansion of Đại Việt into Cham territories, preserving Champa's independence for a critical period.", "He fostered a unique form of religious syncretism within Ch